# Revising against feedback (surgical modification)

The downstream tier (impl) already exists and was composed against the
techspec, pubapi, privapi, processes, and dependencies sections in the
body today. Anything the feedback doesn't touch must round-trip
identically — otherwise the impl underneath this sub goes stale and
forces a regen the author didn't ask for. The CLI re-validates on
write.

## The preserve-don't-redesign discipline

* **Every section is independently sticky.** Each feeds a specific downstream reader (impl's plan, sibling subs' contract, parent comp's roll-up). Touch only the section(s) the feedback names. The other sections stay verbatim.
* **The pubapi section is the load-bearing handle.** It's what impl reads to know what to implement and what callers (sibling subs, dependent comps' subs) read to know what's available. Edit pubapi only when the feedback asks; preserve signature shapes, error variants, and naming conventions if you're not asked to touch them.
* **The privapi section is the sub's internal contract with itself.** Impl reads it to know the helper surface; touch it only when the feedback is about internal articulation.
<!-- voided: source additionally named a sticky "internal-structure" section, distinct from privapi — this tier has no such section; internal structure is what privapi already is, so the bullet above covers it -->
* **Processes and dependencies are sticky.** The process inventory is what impl reads to know which named processes to register, under which category, with which guardrails; the dependency list is what impl reads to know which sibling subs it may call into. Touch either only when the feedback names a process or a dependency edge specifically.
<!-- voided: source also listed "failure-surface and policies" as sticky commitments — this tier has neither section (failure modes route through pubapi; policy reachability is transitive from the parent comp's policies), so there is nothing to preserve here beyond what the pubapi bullet already covers -->

## When the feedback says…

* **"Add an operation to the pubapi"** — edit only the pubapi section. Don't re-litigate the techspec or privapi unless the new operation visibly cuts across them.
* **"Tighten the privapi"** — edit only privapi. Pubapi stays verbatim.
* **"Add a process"** or **"add a guardrail to process X"** — edit only the processes section, and within it, only the named process the feedback identifies. Leave every other process entry untouched.
* **"Add a dependency on sub X"** or **"drop the dependency on sub X"** — edit only the dependencies section. Ground the change in the techspec or pubapi/privapi prose per the reconciliation-pass rule above; don't leave an ungrounded `<dep>` behind.
* **"Address review finding #N"** — pull the prior review text, map the finding to one or two sections, modify only those.
<!-- voided: source's "Add a failure mode" → edit failure_surface, and "Re-articulate internal structure" → edit internal_structure — neither section exists at this tier -->

## Anti-patterns

* Re-articulating the techspec to "tighten" it when the feedback was about the pubapi. The techspec is a hand-off from the parent comparch's techspec; re-articulating it on a sub-level edit creates handle drift the author didn't ask for.
* "Polishing" pubapi signatures across the board on a privapi-focused edit. Pubapi changes propagate to every dependent sub's privapi — keep them tightly scoped.
